Q: Inserting node at the end of a list A: <p>The function is broken indeed, but not for a list with a single node: it has undefined behavior when passed a null pointer, ie: an empty string.</p>
<p>Here is why:</p>
<ul>
<li>if <code>head</code> is null, <code>current</code> is initialized to a null pointer, and the test <code>if (current == NULL)</code> is true</li>
<li><code>new = head</code> sets <code>new</code> to a null pointer, losing the pointer to the allocated block (memory leak)</li>
<li><code>new-&gt;next = NULL</code> dereferences the null pointer, which has undefined behavior and most likely causes the program to end with a segmentation fault.</li>
</ul>
<p>Remove the <code>new = head;</code> assignment to fix the problem.</p>
<p>Here is a modified and simplified version:</p>
<pre><code>// Append a new node containing value at the end of the linked list.
// The head of the new list is returned.
struct node *append_node(int value, struct node *head) {
    struct node *new_node = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    new_node-&gt;data = value; 
    new_node-&gt;next = NULL;

    if (head == NULL) {
        head = new_node;
    } else {
        struct node *current = head;
        while (current-&gt;next != NULL) {
            current = current-&gt;next;
        }
        current-&gt;next = new_node;
    }
    return head; 
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an alternative using a classic C technique to avoid making a special case of the empty list:</p>
<pre><code>struct node *append_node(int value, struct node *head) {
    struct node **tailp = &amp;head;
    while (*tailp) {
        tailp = &amp;(*tailp)-&gt;next;
    }
    struct node *new_node = malloc(sizeof(*new_node));
    new_node-&gt;data = value; 
    new_node-&gt;next = NULL;
    *tailp = new_node;
    return head; 
}
</code></pre>

Q: CSS click to expand menu, instead of hover A: <p>A simple approach that uses HTML and CSS only is based on the `:target` pseudo-class:</p>
<p><a href="http://jsfiddle.net/yucca42/aV5HE/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jsfiddle</a></p>
<p>However, the <code>:target</code> pseudo-class is not supported by IE 8 and older, so if you wish to cover them as well, you need to use some JavaScript polyfill such as <a href="http://code.google.com/p/ie7-js/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IE7.js</a> or Selectivizr (or use an entirely JavaScript-based approach).</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#cssmenu li ul {
  display: none;
}

#cssmenu :target ul {
  display: block;
}

li:hover ul {
  display: block;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;h1&gt;menu with target&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ul id="cssmenu"&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;a id=sub1 href=#sub1&gt;submenu 1
      &lt;ul&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;foo&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;bar&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;a id=sub2 href=#sub2&gt;submenu 2
      &lt;ul&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;item A&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;item B&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;item C&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
